# Early Detection of OOMKilled Issues

## Goal
Catch memory problems before they cause a crash, not after.

## 1. Metrics to watch
- Memory usage vs memory limit (%) — the most important one
- Restart count per container — shows if OOMKilled already happened
- Memory usage trend over time — a steady climb points to a memory leak
- Error rate and response latency — often rise before a full crash

## 2. Alerts to set up
```
Warning:  memory usage > 65% for 5 minutes  → notify team
Critical: memory usage > 85% for 2 minutes  → page on-call
Crash loop: restart count +2 within 10 minutes → urgent alert
```

Example (Prometheus/PromQL):
```promql
(container_memory_working_set_bytes / container_spec_memory_limit_bytes) > 0.65
(container_memory_working_set_bytes / container_spec_memory_limit_bytes) > 0.85
increase(kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total[10m]) > 2
```

## 3. Spotting a memory leak
Watch the *rate* of memory growth, not just the current value. If memory keeps climbing even during low traffic and never drops, that's a leak — not just normal load.

## 4. Dashboard
Put these on one dashboard: memory usage per container over time (with the limit as a line), restart counts, and memory usage next to request rate (to tell load spikes apart from real leaks).

## Summary
